+# Contract: internal Python API
+
+**Feature**: `001-expense-splitter` | **Date**: 2026-11-03
+
+**Status: internal.** The normative, user-facing interface is [`cli.md`](./cli.md). These signatures
+exist so the test suite can assert the cent-conservation invariants of FR-014/SC-002 directly on
+pure functions instead of inferring them from printed text (Constitution III). They are not a
+published library API and may change freely as long as `cli.md` keeps holding.
+
+Types follow [data-model.md](../data-model.md). All money is `int` cents.
+
+## `splitter.errors`
+
+```python
+class SplitterError(Exception):
+    """An input or validation failure whose message is ready to show the user."""
+```
+
+The single exception type for every exit-1 rejection. Carries a finished message with no
+`splitter: error: ` prefix — `cli.main` adds that. Raised by `cli.parse_participants` and by
+`expenses.read_expenses`; caught only in `cli.main` (research D8).
+
+## `splitter.expenses`
+
+```python
+@dataclass(frozen=True)
+class Expense:
+    payer: str          # trimmed; a known participant
+    amount_cents: int   # >= 0
+    line: int           # 1-based physical line, for messages only
+
+HEADER: tuple[str, str, str] = ("payer", "amount", "description")
+
+def parse_amount(value: str, *, path: str, line: int) -> int:
+    """Trim `value`, match `^\\+?\\d+(?:\\.\\d{1,2})?$`, return whole cents.
+
+    Raises SplitterError naming path, line, and value if it does not match. (FR-003, FR-004)
+    """
+
+def read_expenses(path: str, participants: Sequence[str]) -> list[Expense]:
+    """Read and fully validate the expenses file; descriptions are discarded.
+
+    Opens with encoding="utf-8-sig", newline="". Skips blank rows. Requires the first
+    non-blank row to equal HEADER exactly. Validates each data row in order: field count,
+    payer membership, amount. Returns expenses in file order.
+
+    Raises SplitterError for: unreadable path (FR-009), undecodable bytes (FR-002a),
+    missing or wrong header (FR-002, FR-010), wrong field count (FR-010), unknown payer
+    (FR-006), invalid amount (FR-003, FR-004). Reports only the first problem (FR-010a).
+    """
+```
+
+`participants` is passed in because the payer check (FR-006) is a data-row error that must be
+ordered among the other row errors by line number (FR-010a).
+
+## `splitter.settle`
+
+Pure functions — no I/O, no exceptions raised, no global state.
+
+```python
+@dataclass(frozen=True)
+class Payment:
+    payer: str
+    payee: str
+    amount_cents: int   # >= 1
+
+def compute_shares(participants: Sequence[str], total_cents: int) -> dict[str, int]:
+    """Equal split with the remainder charged one cent each to the first `remainder`
+    participants in ascending code-point name order.
+
+    Postcondition: sum(result.values()) == total_cents exactly. (FR-011, FR-011a)
+    """
+
+def compute_balances(
+    participants: Sequence[str], expenses: Sequence[Expense]
+) -> dict[str, int]:
+    """Each participant's paid total minus their share. Participants absent from every
+    expense row are included with a paid total of 0.
+
+    Postcondition: sum(result.values()) == 0 exactly. (FR-012)
+    """
+
+def settle(balances: Mapping[str, int]) -> list[Payment]:
+    """Greedy largest-debt against largest-credit, re-selected each step, ties broken in
+    ascending code-point name order. Returns payments in generation order.
+
+    Preconditions: sum(balances.values()) == 0.
+    Postconditions:
+      len(result) <= len(balances) - 1                                  (FR-013)
+      all(p.amount_cents >= 1 for p in result)                          (FR-013)
+      sum(p.amount_cents) == sum(b for b in balances.values() if b > 0) (FR-014)
+      applying every payment leaves all balances exactly 0              (SC-002)
+      fully determined by `balances`                                    (FR-015, SC-005)
+    """
+```
+
+`compute_shares` takes `total_cents` rather than the expenses so the share arithmetic of FR-011 can
+be tested over totals and group sizes without constructing files; `settle` takes a balance map for
+the same reason.
+
+## `splitter.cli`
+
+```python
+def parse_participants(raw: str) -> list[str]:
+    """Split on commas, trim each element, return names in command-line order.
+
+    Raises SplitterError for: no names at all, any empty or whitespace-only element,
+    any duplicate. (FR-007, FR-008)
+    """
+
+def format_payment(payment: Payment) -> str:
+    """Render as 'PAYER pays PAYEE AMOUNT' with two decimal places, by integer
+    arithmetic — never float formatting. (FR-016)
+    """
+
+def main(argv: Sequence[str] | None = None) -> int:
+    """Entry point. Returns 0 on success, 1 on SplitterError. argparse exits 2 itself on a
+    malformed command line.
+
+    Writes payment lines to stdout only after all validation has succeeded, so stdout is
+    untouched on failure (FR-019). Writes one 'splitter: error: {message}' line to stderr
+    on SplitterError. `argv` defaults to sys.argv[1:]; tests pass it explicitly to exercise
+    the CLI in process (research D11).
+    """
+```
+
+Returning an `int` rather than calling `sys.exit` is what lets most CLI tests run in process; only
+`__main__.py` turns the value into a process status.
+
+## `splitter.__main__`
+
+```python
+raise SystemExit(main())
+```
+
+Nothing else — this module exists so `python -m splitter` works, as the spec's Given Constraints
+require.
